<article xml:id="document" label="document">
  <title>Intermediate Algebra (AA) - Course Syllabus</title>

  <introduction>
    <p>Palm Beach State College Course Syllabus — Fall 2026 MAT 1033C Intermediate Algebra (AA)</p>
  </introduction>

  <section xml:id="general-class-info">
    <title>General Class and Course Information</title>
    <p>This syllabus covers multiple sections of MAT 1033C Intermediate Algebra (AA). Update the term and section-specific meeting information below as needed each term.</p>
    <ul>
      <li><p>Course Title: Intermediate Algebra (AA)</p></li>
      <li><p>Credits/Clock Hours: 4.00</p></li>
      <li><p>Term/Date: Fall 2026, 08/21/2026 - 12/10/2026</p></li>
      <li><p>Room: CB 0202 (Boca Raton)</p></li>
    </ul>
    <p>Sections covered by this syllabus:</p>
    <ul>
      <li><p>Course Section: MAT 1033C 170 — Meeting Times: Mon/Wed/Fri, 12:30PM - 1:45PM, 08/21/26 - 12/10/26</p></li>
      <li><p>Course Section: MAT 1033C 171 — Meeting Times: Mon/Wed/Fri, 8:00AM - 9:15AM, 08/21/26 - 12/10/26</p></li>
      <li><p>Course Section: MAT 1033C 97 — Meeting Times: Mon/Wed/Fri, 9:30AM - 10:45AM, 08/21/26 - 12/10/26</p></li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      Course Description: Prerequisite — Non-Exempt students will need to provide appropriate placement scores or MAT0022 or MAT0056 or MAT0015 (with a grade of C or higher).
    </p>
    <p>
      This course prepares students for MAC1105. Topics include functions, linear equations and inequalities, exponents and radicals, products and factoring, algebraic fractions and quadratic equations. MAT1033C combines classwork with at least 50 minutes of in-class guided practice each week to develop critical thinking and problem-solving strategies. MAT1033C is NOT a Gordon Rule course and does NOT satisfy part of the math requirements for graduation; it counts as elective credit only.
    </p>
    <p>
      This is a face-to-face course that uses an online component to augment classroom learning, and requires regular on-campus meetings and access to an internet-connected computer.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section xml:id="instructor-info">
    <title>Instructor's Information</title>
    <ul>
      <li><p>Name: Anurag Katyal</p></li>
      <li><p>Office Location: CB 0224</p></li>
      <li><p>Telephone: (561) 862-4530</p></li>
      <li><p>Faculty Webpage: <url href="https://www.palmbeachstate.edu/pf/Faculty.aspx?id=katyala" visual="https://www.palmbeachstate.edu/pf/Faculty.aspx?id=katyala"/></p></li>
      <li><p>Email: <url href="mailto:katyala@pbsc.edu" visual="katyala@pbsc.edu"/></p></li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <section xml:id="textbooks-materials">
    <title>Other Information on Textbooks and Materials Needed for Class</title>
    <p>This is a draft syllabus and may be updated before the start of the Fall 2026 term.</p>
    <p>This course does not require a purchased textbook; free and open-source materials will be used.</p>
    <p>To complete this course, students will need:</p>
    <ul>
      <li><p>A laptop or desktop computer with administrative rights running Windows or Mac OS (Chromebooks not permitted).</p></li>
      <li><p>A scientific calculator — the TI 30XIIS is the only permitted calculator; no other device may be used as a calculator on assessments or in class.</p></li>
      <li><p>Two 70-page spiral-bound notebooks.</p></li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <section xml:id="learning-outcomes">
    <title>Course Learning Outcomes</title>
    <ul>
      <li><p>Graph and interpret graphs in the Cartesian coordinate system and develop equations for lines.</p></li>
      <li><p>Identify domain and range of relations; determine whether a relation is a function.</p></li>
      <li><p>Solve and graph linear systems of equations and inequalities.</p></li>
      <li><p>Solve equations and applications using the zero factor property.</p></li>
      <li><p>Solve quadratic equations and equations reducible to quadratic, including those with complex number solutions.</p></li>
      <li><p>Solve rational and radical equations and applications.</p></li>
      <li><p>Use the laws of exponents to simplify expressions with rational exponents.</p></li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <section xml:id="class-info">
    <title>Class Information</title>
    <p>Attendance on the first day of class is mandatory. Any student who misses the first day or first week, for any reason including late registration, is responsible for the absence counting toward the attendance policy and for work assigned on the first day; this may not be used as an excuse for a make-up or extension.</p>
    <p>Attendance is taken precisely at the start of each class meeting, held in person on all meeting days. Students must watch videos and complete pre-class preparation before attending, and should plan ahead for internet outages, device failures, and other technical emergencies; no help will be provided with technical issues in the 24 hours before assignments are due.</p>
    <p>Students must be able to watch videos with sound and open/edit PDF files and documents locally or in the cloud.</p>
    <p>Outside of class, students should expect to spend a minimum of 3 hours per hour of class time (about 12 hours per week), with 14-16 hours recommended for students who struggled with prerequisites. Students are expected to keep a well-organized notebook and write neat, well-formatted solutions with correct notation.</p>
  </section>

  <section xml:id="assignments-schedule">
    <title>Assignments, Tests, Quizzes and Final Exam Schedule, with Percentage/Points of Grade</title>
    <p>A tentative schedule is provided separately; dates are subject to change and will be communicated on Canvas and Discord.</p>
    <p>Grading is based on mastery/standards-based grading rather than a single-attempt model: students have 3 attempts to show proficiency on each topic via regularly paced quizzes. The course is split into 15 Learning Targets (LTs), 10 of which are marked CORE and are necessary prerequisites for College Algebra.</p>
    <p>Learning Targets:</p>
    <ul>
      <li><p>(LT 1) Explain and justify what it means for a number or object to be a solution to an equation or inequality or their systems.</p></li>
      <li><p>(LT 2, CORE) Formulate, solve, and interpret linear equations, and evaluate solutions in context.</p></li>
      <li><p>(LT 3) Determine, represent, and interpret equations of lines, and analyze how slopes and intercepts describe relationships.</p></li>
      <li><p>(LT 4, CORE) Analyze and solve absolute value equations, represent their solutions, and justify reasoning.</p></li>
      <li><p>(LT 5, CORE) Model, construct, and solve systems of linear equations from word problems, and evaluate the reasonableness of solutions.</p></li>
      <li><p>(LT 6) Solve, represent, and interpret linear inequalities in one and two variables, and compare different forms of solution sets.</p></li>
      <li><p>(LT 7, CORE) Apply and evaluate the properties of exponents to simplify expressions, and classify and construct polynomials by degree and number of terms.</p></li>
      <li><p>(LT 8) Perform, verify, and connect operations on polynomials to broader algebraic structures.</p></li>
      <li><p>(LT 9, CORE) Select, apply, and justify appropriate factoring techniques for polynomials.</p></li>
      <li><p>(LT 10, CORE) Construct and solve equations by factoring, and evaluate solutions for accuracy and context.</p></li>
      <li><p>(LT 11) Perform, simplify, and analyze operations on rational expressions.</p></li>
      <li><p>(LT 12, CORE) Construct, solve, and interpret rational equations, and evaluate potential restrictions on solutions.</p></li>
      <li><p>(LT 13, CORE) Perform, simplify, and justify operations on radical expressions.</p></li>
      <li><p>(LT 14, CORE) Construct and solve radical equations, and evaluate solutions to identify extraneous roots.</p></li>
      <li><p>(LT 15, CORE) Perform and justify operations on complex numbers and analyze and solve quadratic equations with complex solutions.</p></li>
      <li><p>(LT 16) Marked absent six or fewer times throughout the semester in accordance with the Attendance Policy.</p></li>
    </ul>
    <p>Quizzes: 15 short quizzes (roughly 3-15 minutes each), graded proficient/not-yet with no partial credit. Students who reach proficiency do not need to retake; up to four retakes are allowed during designated office hours within a calendar week of grades being released (except quizzes 14 and 15, for which the final exam serves as the retake). Retake quizzes may differ in problems but cover the same learning target.</p>
    <p>Final Exam: A cumulative final exam is given in two parts, on Monday 12/07/2026 and Wednesday 12/09/2026. Both parts must be taken to pass the course; missing a part or scoring below 35% results in an F. There is no make-up or retake for the final exam.</p>
    <p>All work must be shown on all assessments to receive credit; credit depends on correct, clear, logical presentation with proper units, notation, and labeled functions. A correct answer with no or incorrect work, or disorganized work, will not receive credit.</p>
  </section>

  <section xml:id="grading">
    <title>Grading</title>
    <p>A detailed explanation of the grading scheme is provided separately.</p>
    <table>
      <title>Letter Grade Requirements</title>
      <tabular>
        <row header="yes">
          <cell>Grade</cell>
          <cell>Requirement</cell>
        </row>
        <row><cell>A</cell><cell>10 (CORE) LTs and 4, 5, or 6 other LTs</cell></row>
        <row><cell>B</cell><cell>10 (CORE) LTs and 2 or 3 other LTs</cell></row>
        <row><cell>C</cell><cell>10 (CORE) LTs and 1 other LT; or 10 (CORE) LTs; or 9 (CORE) LTs and 4, 5, or 6 other LTs; or 8 (CORE) LTs and 5 or 6 other LTs</cell></row>
        <row><cell>D</cell><cell>9 (CORE) LTs and 1, 2, or 3 other LTs; or 9 (CORE) LTs; or 8 (CORE) LTs and 1, 2, 3, or 4 other LTs; or 8 (CORE) LTs</cell></row>
        <row><cell>F</cell><cell>Mastered 7 or fewer (CORE) LTs</cell></row>
      </tabular>
    </table>
    <p>Impact of Final Exam on Course Grade:</p>
    <ul>
      <li><p>Score above 85%: grade goes up by up to 2 letter grades.</p></li>
      <li><p>Score 70%-84.99%: grade goes up by up to 1 letter grade.</p></li>
      <li><p>Score 60%-69.99%: grade does not change.</p></li>
      <li><p>Score 50%-59.99%: grade goes down by up to 1 letter grade.</p></li>
      <li><p>Score below 50%: grade goes down by up to 2 letter grades.</p></li>
      <li><p>Score below 35%: grade goes down by up to 3 letter grades.</p></li>
    </ul>
    <p>A widget is available to calculate grades and determine what is needed to achieve a particular grade in the course.</p>
  </section>

  <section xml:id="attendance-policy">
    <title>Attendance Policy</title>
    <p>Students are expected to attend all courses and course activities for which they are registered. Students marked absent more than six times will not be able to gain proficiency in (LT 16). Attendance is taken precisely at the start of class.</p>
    <ul>
      <li><p>Any student not present at the start of class when their name is called will be marked absent; there are no tardies in this class.</p></li>
      <li><p>Any student who leaves before class is over will be marked absent.</p></li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <section xml:id="electronic-device-policy">
    <title>Electronic Device Policy</title>
    <p>Students must be able to watch videos with sound and have access to a device that can open PDF files and edit documents locally or in the cloud. Completing daily preparation before class is vital to succeeding in this course.</p>
  </section>

  <section xml:id="late-assignment-policy">
    <title>Late Assignment Policy</title>
    <p>No late work will be accepted for any reason.</p>
  </section>

  <section xml:id="makeup-exam-policy">
    <title>Make-up Exam Policy</title>
    <ol>
      <li><p>There are no make-ups for any assignment.</p></li>
      <li><p>If a student misses a quiz for any reason, they lose that attempt but remain eligible for the retake; proficiency on the final exam for the missing LT will also be given credit.</p></li>
    </ol>
  </section>

  <section xml:id="academic-dishonesty-policy">
    <title>Academic Dishonesty Policy</title>
    <p>Academic dishonesty includes actions aimed at making false representation with respect to a student's academic performance, such as cheating on an exam, unauthorized collaboration, submitting work previously submitted in another course, knowingly assisting another student in dishonest conduct, and plagiarism.</p>
  </section>

  <section xml:id="academic-dishonesty-penalty">
    <title>Academic Dishonesty Penalty for Violation</title>
    <p>Academic dishonesty will result in a failing grade for this class. Students may not withdraw from the course to avoid a failing grade.</p>
  </section>

  <section xml:id="withdrawal-policy">
    <title>Withdrawal Policy</title>
    <p>A student may be withdrawn by the professor for excessive absences after being marked absent for the 6th class meeting.</p>
  </section>

  <section xml:id="accessibility-statement">
    <title>Accessibility Statement</title>
    <p>Students with disabilities are advised, in compliance with federal and state laws, that accommodations and services are available through the Center for Student Accessibility (CSA). It is the student's responsibility to contact CSA advisors and submit appropriate documentation prior to receiving accommodations or services.</p>
  </section>

  <section xml:id="face-to-face-info">
    <title>Face-to-Face Class Information</title>
    <p>This course meets on campus during regularly scheduled class time and is supported/supplemented by the Canvas Learning Management System.</p>

    <subsection xml:id="canvas-access">
      <title>Canvas Course Access</title>
      <p>Access to the Canvas Course Support site is available (read-only) 48 hours before the first day of class each term.</p>
    </subsection>

    <subsection xml:id="canvas-orientation">
      <title>Canvas Orientation and Assistance</title>
      <p>Students can register for the free Canvas Online Orientation Course (ELO1000 Passport to Canvas) and obtain assistance from the Canvas Student Support Center.</p>
    </subsection>

    <subsection xml:id="canvas-tips">
      <title>Canvas Tips and Tutorials</title>
      <p>Students should ensure their computer is compatible, understand the examination proctoring and plagiarism detection systems that may be used, and learn the functions of Canvas.</p>
    </subsection>

    <subsection xml:id="distance-learning-req">
      <title>Distance Learning Requirements</title>
      <p>Computer systems must be compatible with Canvas; system requirements and a system check are available online. Students without home computer access may use SLC Computer Labs and Libraries at Palm Beach State campuses. Students need basic web-browsing skills and can learn Canvas functions through the free orientation course and video tutorials.</p>
      <p>For Canvas Course Support Site assistance, students should contact their professor for instructional content issues, Canvas Support for functional/feature questions, or the Student IT Service Desk for username/password and access issues.</p>
    </subsection>
  </section>

  <section xml:id="student-code-of-conduct">
    <title>Student Code of Conduct</title>
    <p>Original work based on students' own perspectives is expected. The use of PhotoMath, OpenAI, LLMs, ChatGPT, and/or similar programs to create content or solve problems for assignments is not permitted and will result in a failing grade (F) for the course.</p>
  </section>

  <section xml:id="eab-navigate">
    <title>EAB Navigate - Student Success Platform</title>
    <p>Navigate connects students to resources needed for success, such as academic advising, the SLC, the Career Center, technology support, and alerts issued by instructors and advisors, accessible via the MyPBSCApp.</p>
  </section>

  <section xml:id="college-policies">
    <title>College Policies</title>

    <subsection xml:id="academic-dishonesty-integrity">
      <title>Academic Dishonesty/Integrity</title>
      <p>Academic dishonesty includes, but is not limited to, unauthorized use of aids, cheating, fabrication, plagiarism, or facilitating academic dishonesty in the classroom or other college environments.</p>
    </subsection>

    <subsection xml:id="plagiarism">
      <title>Plagiarism</title>
      <p>Plagiarism includes taking another's words or substance without credit, submitting work written by someone else, submitting the same or slightly altered paper for more than one course (self-plagiarism), failing to credit ideas or facts derived from another, failing to use quotation marks when quoting directly, and extended paraphrasing of another's work.</p>
    </subsection>

    <subsection xml:id="ai-plagiarism">
      <title>Plagiarism - Use of Artificial Intelligence (AI) Programs</title>
      <p>AI programs can produce writing resembling human work; presenting AI-completed work as the student's own violates the College's plagiarism standards and constitutes academic dishonesty, subject to the penalties described in the Student Handbook, unless properly cited with explicit instructor permission.</p>
    </subsection>

    <subsection xml:id="cheating">
      <title>Cheating</title>
      <p>Cheating includes using unauthorized notes or aids on an exam, altering graded work before resubmitting for regrading, allowing another person to complete and submit work under one's own name, and unauthorized collaboration or sharing of assignment-specific information.</p>
    </subsection>

    <subsection xml:id="fabrication">
      <title>Fabrication</title>
      <p>Fabrication is presenting data not gathered according to appropriate guidelines, or failing to accurately describe how data were collected or generated.</p>
    </subsection>

    <subsection xml:id="aiding-abetting">
      <title>Aiding and Abetting Dishonesty</title>
      <p>Providing material or information to another person knowing it will be used improperly, pursuant to Fla. Stat. 877.17 (2011).</p>
    </subsection>

    <subsection xml:id="forgery">
      <title>Forgery</title>
      <p>Altering academic records, forging a signature of authorization, or falsifying information on official academic documents. Students may not withdraw from a class to avoid a failing grade resulting from such a violation.</p>
    </subsection>

    <subsection xml:id="classroom-etiquette">
      <title>Classroom Etiquette and Student Behavior Guidelines</title>
      <p>Students are expected to show respect for professors and fellow students; disruptive behavior will be addressed by the professor and may lead to formal conduct charges and sanctions under the Student Handbook.</p>
    </subsection>

    <subsection xml:id="computer-competency">
      <title>Computer Competency Component</title>
      <p>Each student will demonstrate a fundamental understanding of basic computer operations through professor-determined exercises and/or assignments.</p>
    </subsection>

    <subsection xml:id="csa">
      <title>Center for Student Accessibility</title>
      <p>Accommodations and services are available through the CSA; it is the student's responsibility to contact CSA and submit documentation prior to receiving accommodations.</p>
    </subsection>

    <subsection xml:id="eating-drinking-smoking">
      <title>Eating, Drinking and Smoking</title>
      <p>Eating and drinking are confined to designated campus areas. Smoking, including e-cigarettes, is not permitted in College buildings and only in designated outdoor areas.</p>
    </subsection>

    <subsection xml:id="grade-appeal">
      <title>Final Course Grade Appeal</title>
      <p>Evaluation of academic work and the method for assigning final grades is the responsibility of the instructor and must be communicated in writing in the syllabus prior to the start of the course; grade questions follow procedures in the Student Handbook.</p>
    </subsection>

    <subsection xml:id="midterm-grades">
      <title>Mid-Term Grade Reporting</title>
      <p>Mid-term grades will be made available to students within one week after the close of the mid-term grading period.</p>
    </subsection>

    <subsection xml:id="intellectual-recording">
      <title>Intellectual Recording - HB233</title>
      <p>Students may record video or audio of class lectures for personal educational use without prior notice, but recording other class activities (discussions, presentations, exams, private conversations, etc.) or other students' images/voices is prohibited; recordings may not substitute for attendance and may not be shared without instructor consent.</p>
    </subsection>

    <subsection xml:id="student-responsibility">
      <title>Student Responsibility Policy</title>
      <p>By enrolling, students become subject to the College's jurisdiction and pledge to follow its rules and regulations, including preparing for class, participating, and completing assignments on time.</p>
    </subsection>

    <subsection xml:id="websites-of-interest">
      <title>Palm Beach State Websites of Interest</title>
      <p>A list of relevant web addresses for students is available through the College library page.</p>
    </subsection>

    <subsection xml:id="final-withdrawal-policy">
      <title>Withdrawal Policy</title>
      <p>The last day to withdraw from a course with a "W" grade is 2026-10-27. It is the responsibility of the student to complete a withdrawal request online through Workday; an official withdrawal entitles the student to a grade of "W" in the course.</p>
    </subsection>
  </section>

